    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>In an increasingly loud, bright and fast-paced world, the bedroom should be more than just a place to sleep; it should be a calm retreat. For many people, including autistic people, people with ADHD or sensory processing differences, and anyone who finds noise, bright light or busy rooms tiring, the surroundings make a real difference to how easy it is to relax. A sensory-friendly bedroom is designed to reduce sensory overload while maximising comfort. By intentionally curating what we see, touch, hear and smell, we can turn a standard room into a space that makes rest easier.</p>
<p>A note before you start: this is design advice, not therapy. Everyone’s sensory needs are different. Some people want a quiet, muted room; others feel calmer with firm pressure, movement or a favourite texture, and many are a mix of both. If you are planning a room for a child or adult with specific sensory needs, an occupational therapist can assess those needs and advise on what is likely to help.</p>

<h2 id="lighting">1. Lighting</h2>
<!--figure:bedroom-pendant-lights-cove-ceiling|Soft, indirect cove lighting, low pendants and sheer curtains give this neutral bedroom a gentle, glare-free glow.-->
<p>Lighting is often the biggest sensory issue in a bedroom. Harsh overhead fluorescent lights or cool, blue-toned LEDs can feel glaring and uncomfortable, especially in the evening. To create a sensory-friendly space, the goal is layered lighting.</p>
<p>Layered lighting lets you control the intensity of light based on your needs at the time. Start with blackout curtains or blinds: darkness supports sleep for many people, and a blackout blind inside the window recess, layered with blackout-lined curtains that extend 15–20 cm (6–8 in) past each side of the frame, keeps light leaking around the edges to a minimum. For the evening, swap standard bulbs for dimmable or smart bulbs that let you adjust the colour temperature; warm, amber tones (around 2700K) feel softer and more restful than cool white. Some people are sensitive to the flicker of certain LED bulbs, so try a few and choose ones labelled flicker-free where available. Small, unobtrusive light sources such as a salt lamp or other low lamp, LED strips hidden under the bed frame, or motion-sensor floor lighting give enough light for getting up in the night without a jolt of brightness. If you have pets, keep salt lamps out of reach, as they can be harmful if licked.</p>

<h2 id="textures">2. Textures</h2>
<!--figure:curved-upholstered-bed|A curved boucle bed, textured plaster wall and woven rattan pendant bring soft, tactile texture to this bedroom.-->
<p>The sense of touch is closely tied to how safe and comfortable we feel. In a sensory-friendly bedroom, the choice of fabric can be the difference between a restless night and a comfortable one. Many people find the gentle pressure of a weighted blanket calming, a little like a firm hug. Choose an appropriate weight, check the maker’s guidance, and note that weighted blankets aren’t suitable for young children; ask an occupational therapist if you are unsure.</p>
<p>Texture is highly personal. Some find the coolness of bamboo-derived or silk sheets soothing, while others prefer the warmth and softness of flannel or fleece. Remove irritating elements such as scratchy lace, stiff seams, itchy wool and labels. Consider the feel of every item that touches your skin, from the rug you step on in the morning to the pyjamas you wear at night.</p>

<h2 id="colours">3. Colours</h2>
<p>Visual noise is real. Bright, high-contrast colours and busy patterns can feel overstimulating and tiring to look at. When designing a sensory-friendly space, lean towards a low-arousal colour palette: muted, earthy tones such as sage green, dusty blue, soft greys and warm taupes.</p>
<p>Many people find these colours calming and restful. Avoid high-gloss paint, which reflects light and creates glare; choose matt or eggshell finishes instead. Painting walls, woodwork and even the ceiling in similar tones keeps contrast low. Keeping the walls fairly bare, or choosing simple, nature-inspired art, also gives the eye less to process, so the room feels quieter.</p>

<h2 id="sound">4. Sound</h2>
<p>The sound environment is often the hardest to control, especially in towns and cities. A sensory-friendly bedroom deals with both keeping unwanted noise down and adding soothing sound where it helps. Soft decor does a lot of the work: heavy rugs, velvet or interlined curtains and upholstered headboards look beautiful and absorb sound, reducing echoes within the room. Felt pads on furniture legs and an oiled door hinge take care of the small, irritating noises.</p>
<p>For outside noise such as traffic or neighbours, a white noise machine or a fan provides a steady curtain of sound that masks sudden noises. Some people prefer pink or brown noise, which have more low frequencies and can feel less sharp than white noise. Others need silence, so keep earplugs or ear defenders in a set place. Whatever works, consistency helps: a predictable sound environment is easier to relax in. Bear in mind that decor softens sound but won’t soundproof a room; for serious noise problems, speak to an acoustic specialist.</p>

<h2 id="organisation">5. Organisation</h2>
<p>Clutter is a major source of visual overstimulation. In a sensory-friendly bedroom, “out of sight, out of mind” is a useful principle. A cluttered room keeps catching the eye, which makes it harder to switch off. Built-in or integrated storage that hides personal items, books and electronics makes a big difference.</p>
<p>Choose furniture with clean lines and closed cupboards rather than open shelving, and hide cables where you can. Picture or word labels on drawers and boxes make it easier to find things and put them back. Dedicated zones help too: keeping a workspace or exercise equipment out of the bedroom, or at least behind a folding screen, keeps the sleep space feeling separate from work and high-energy activity. Clear floors and clear surfaces make a calmer room.</p>

<h2 id="scent">6. Scent</h2>
<p>Scent can have a strong effect on how a room feels, but the key in a sensory-friendly bedroom is subtlety. Strong, artificial fragrances from candles or plug-in air fresheners can be overwhelming and may irritate people with allergies, asthma or scent sensitivities.</p>
<p>Some people find scents such as lavender, cedarwood, sandalwood or bergamot relaxing; keep it subtle. An ultrasonic diffuser lets you control the intensity and switch it off easily. Take care with essential oils: they can be harmful to pets, particularly cats (see our guide to <a href="/blog/non-toxic-decor-for-cats">non-toxic decor for cats</a>), and they don’t suit everyone, so ask the people who share the room first. For those who are very sensitive to smell, the goal may simply be neutrality: a well-ventilated room free of lingering odours.</p>

<h2 id="layout">7. Layout</h2>
<p>The layout of the room affects how easy it is to move around and how settled you feel in it. A cramped room full of sharp corners can feel awkward and stressful. A sensory-friendly layout puts flow and safety first.</p>
<p>Leave clear walking routes, ideally around 60–75 cm (24–30 in) wide, and choose furniture with rounded edges to avoid painful bumps. Many people like the bed in a “command position”, where you can see the door without being directly in line with it, as it can feel more settled. Creating a sensory corner with a bean bag or a soft floor cushion gives a designated space for stimming, quiet time or reflection away from the bed, helping to keep the bed for sleep. Anchor tall furniture to the wall, especially in children’s rooms, and keep blind cords out of reach.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Lie on the bed and look around before you finish. What you see last at night and first in the morning should be calm: no glowing standby lights, bright objects or piles of clutter in your line of sight.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A sensory-friendly bedroom isn’t about a particular style; it’s about giving the person who uses the room more control over what they see, hear, feel and smell. Start with the changes that make the biggest difference to them, often light and clutter, involve them in the choices where you can, and adjust as you learn what helps. Small, considered changes add up to a room that feels genuinely restful.</p>

<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="only-for-autism-or-adhd">Is a sensory-friendly bedroom only for autistic people or people with ADHD?</h3>
<p>No. Many neurodivergent people find these changes especially helpful, but anyone can benefit from a calmer bedroom. Plenty of people feel worn out by noise, screens and busy surroundings, and a quieter, softer room can make it easier to unwind and build good sleep habits.</p>
<h3 id="which-textures-are-right">How do I know which textures are right for me?</h3>
<p>Experiment. Visit a fabric shop and touch different materials, or notice which clothes you reach for when you are stressed. If you feel comforted by heavy blankets, you may enjoy firmer pressure; if you find yourself kicking off the covers, you may prefer breathable, lightweight natural fibres such as linen.</p>
<h3 id="white-pink-brown-noise">What is the difference between white, pink and brown noise?</h3>
<p>White noise contains all frequencies at equal intensity, like radio static. Pink noise has more power at lower frequencies and sounds like steady rain. Brown noise is deeper still, like a low roar or distant thunder. Many people with sensory sensitivities find pink or brown noise less hissy than white noise.</p>
<h3 id="on-a-budget">Can I make my room sensory-friendly on a budget?</h3>
<p>Yes. You don’t need expensive gadgets. Start by decluttering, which costs nothing, then switch to warm-toned bulbs and use a heavier spare blanket if you like extra weight on the bed. Covering bright standby lights on electronics with black tape makes a big difference for very little.</p>
<h3 id="partner-different-needs">How do I handle a partner who has different sensory needs?</h3>
<p>Communication and zoning are key. Use separate bedding, such as two single duvets on one bed (often called the Scandinavian sleep method), so each person can choose their own weight and texture. If one person needs sound and the other needs silence, try sleep headphones or good earplugs. Bedside lamps or smart lighting can also be set separately for each side of the bed.</p>

